\paragraph{Weight-decay schedules in detail.}
For $\alpha=\rho=1$ and a copy target the balance \eqref{eq:balance} holds with $\lambda t$ replaced by $\int_0^t\lambda$, so the direction is an exact non-autonomous scalar system.
After a loss $p/w$ rises, is held or falls as $\lambda_W-\alpha\lambda_P$ is positive, zero or negative; the slaved relation $p=w\sqrt{\lambda_W/\lambda_P}$ of the development stage model holds after a loss only for $\alpha\lambda_P=\lambda_W$, and the frozen model replaces it by the larger of the predictor's own decay and its static nullcline.
On the held-out grid the development stage model agrees on \ExtBAgreeVone{} of the changed pairs and capture followed by the static threshold on \ExtBAgreeCapStatic{} (the frozen model's margin in agreement over all pairs where any predictor changes state: CI $\ExtBVoneCI$ and $\ExtBCapStaticCI$) and the static threshold at the current value on \ExtBAgreeStaticNow{}; post-change events have \ExtBUnmatched{} unmatched against \ExtBUnmatchedCapStatic{} for capture-static (90th percentile timing \ExtBTimePninety{}), and the final count errs by \ExtBFinalCapStatic{} under capture-static.
In \ExtBLateZero{} of late-decrease runs nothing is revived; an early decrease is within one of the lower constant in \ExtBEarlyWithin{} of pairs, and in the frozen model itself in \ExtBEarlyModel{}, so that consequence was wrong rather than missed.
The $\mu$ count agrees on \ExtBMuAgree{} of the changed pairs.
The per-cell bar applies to the \ExtBCells{} of the \ExtBCellsAll{} (kind $\times$ variant) cells with at least 100 changed pairs; none of the three failing cells is flagged ill-posed.
Over the grid the truth regains \ExtBRegainTruth{} directions after a loss and the frozen model \ExtBRegainModel{}, \ExtBRegainShared{} of them the same (reported, not scored).
Read post hoc, the short-pulse failure lies in the predictor after a loss, which decides whether a direction lost during the pulse regrows: the sign of the truth's deviation from the slaved relation follows $\mathrm{sign}(\lambda_W-\alpha\lambda_P)$ in every held-out cell, as stated before the score was read, but this is not a scored statistic, and no law for the predictor after a loss has been tested on held-out runs.

\paragraph{Minibatch noise (B3).}
B3 trains the aligned linear JEPA with $n=16$ modes and a copy target by minibatch SGD on fresh Gaussian samples, at batch sizes $B\in\{2,8,32\}$ and with population gradients, over two values each of $\lambda$, $p_0$ and $\eta$ (\ExtBthRuns{} held-out runs, \ExtBthDiverged{} diverged; registration and an amendment of its scoring both before any held-out run; rows in \cref{tab:ext-tests}).
The noise is multiplicative and every term acting on a dead output direction is proportional to it, so in this model reseeding and an additive floor are excluded by construction; S1 (no re-escape: \ExtBthReesc{} of \ExtBthNoisy{} noisy runs) is therefore a consistency check, not evidence against a floor, and the noise clause does not cover a lagging EMA target, additive noise or nonlinear layers.
S2, that noise drives dead directions lower than the population run (median $\log_{10}$ of the largest dead singular value relative to the top: $B=2<B=32<$ population at each $\eta$), fails: at $\eta=0.02$, $B=32$ ends at $\ExtBthSTwoBtt$ against $\ExtBthSTwoPop$.
Split post hoc, the order holds in all \ExtBthSTwoLoHold{} $\lambda\times\eta$ cells at $p_0=0.7$ and fails in all \ExtBthSTwoHiFail{} at $p_0=1.5$, where $W$ first collapses as a whole and leaves the population run's dead directions deepest, at the same levels in float64 and 80-bit state.
S3 is undetermined: noise moves the count by \ExtBthSThree{} directions on average against the population run, inside the bar of $1.5$, but by \ExtBthSThreeSens{} when the diverged run counts as a miss; the shift account of prior work changes no count here (it equals the population race on every noisy run), the count read at $0.1T$ does as well (\ExtBthEarly; difference $\ExtBthEarlyCI$, not resolved), and the numerical exponents (\ExtBthMc), the window and every $\mu$ variant (\ExtBthWindow) and the static bound (\ExtBthStatic) do worse.
S4 passes: the sign of the noise effect follows $p_0$ ($\ExtBthSFourLo$ at $p_0=0.7$, $\ExtBthSFourHi$ at $p_0=1.5$; by $\lambda=0.006, 0.012$: $\ExtBthDLoA$, $\ExtBthDLoB$ and $\ExtBthDHiA$, $\ExtBthDHiB$), replicating the development pattern on held-out runs and unchanged in 80-bit state; no account here predicts its sign or size.
At $p_0=1.5$, \ExtBthRegrow{} directions of noisy runs that the initial collapse takes below $10^{-13}$ of the top singular value grow once their window opens, where registration expected none; they are the same in 80-bit state (\ExtBthRegrowF), where the \ExtBthDepthDeeper{} that bottom at float64's floor are resolved and \ExtBthDepthSameCross{} of \ExtBthDepthEvents{} cross the threshold at the same check, so they are the exact dynamics and not re-escapes, while float32 state, whose resolution lies above those levels, changes \ExtBthFthirtytwo{} of \ExtBthFthirtytwoRuns{} final counts at $p_0=1.5$.
These are statements about this linear model; whether noise that breaks the invariance creates a floor is untested.

\paragraph{Deep linear encoder.}
With identity predictor $p_0I$, copy target and factor amplitudes $w_1,\dots,w_L$ of direction $k$, $\dot w_i=\gamma(p)a^2/w_i-\lambda w_i$ and $\dot p=(c-p\sigma)a^2-\lambda p$, with $a=\prod_iw_i$.
Then $\frac{d}{dt}(w_i^2-w_j^2)=-2\lambda(w_i^2-w_j^2)$ and $\frac{d}{dt}(p^2-w_i^2)=-2\lambda(p^2-w_i^2)$, which give the reduction of \cref{sec:deep}.
With $p_r=p_0e^{-\lambda t}$ and $u=a^m$, $\dot u=mL\gamma u^2-mL\lambda u$, so $v=1/u$ solves a linear equation and $v(t)=e^{(2L-2)\lambda t}\big[a_0^{-m}-(2L-2)I(t)\big]$ with $I(t)=\int_0^t\gamma(p_r)e^{-(2L-2)\lambda\tau}d\tau$; $u$ blows up in finite time, i.e.\ the direction escapes, if and only if \eqref{eq:deep} holds.
A1 drops $a^{2/L}$ beside $p_0^2$ in $p$, which is not small for $L=3$ near threshold.
The $\mu$ count of \citet{demelocosta2026drive}, applied to the end-to-end map, equals the initial window on all \ExtCRuns{} runs (error \ExtCMuAl{} aligned and \ExtCMuGe{} generic for $L\ge2$; added after the score, from inputs only); linearised literally at $W_1=\dots=W_L=0$ it is degenerate for $L\ge2$, since every gradient term in $W_i$ carries the other factors.
On the informative runs (\ExtCAlInfRuns{} aligned, \ExtCGeInfRuns{} generic; post hoc) A1 errs by \ExtCAlInfMae{} and \ExtCGeInfMae{}, the reading at $t=50$ by \ExtCAlInfEarly{} and \ExtCGeInfEarly{}.

\paragraph{Deep predictor with random factors (rung 1b).}
With a depth-one encoder and a predictor $P=Q_M\cdots Q_1$ whose factors are arbitrary, weight decay $\lambda$ on every factor and a copy target, the predictor's gradient near capture is the rank-one $-(c-p_u\sigma)a^2uu^\top$ along the latent direction $u$ of the growing mode, so $p_u=u^\top Pu$ obeys $\dot p_u=\alpha[(c-p_u\sigma)a^2K_u-M\lambda p_u]$ with $K_u=\sum_{j=1}^M|Q_{>j}^\top u|^2|Q_{<j}u|^2$ ($Q_{>j}=Q_M\cdots Q_{j+1}$, $Q_{<j}=Q_{j-1}\cdots Q_1$), and the mode captures the predictor once $a^2(c-p_u\sigma)K_u\ge M\lambda p_u$.
For identity factors $K_u=Mp^{2-2/M}$ and this is the E1c correction (\cref{app:more}); for $M=1$ it is the depth-one rule.
Before capture each factor only decays, so $K_u(t)=e^{-2(M-1)\alpha\lambda t}K_u(0)$; the rule is applied to each singular triplet of the integrated linear system with the actual $W_0$ and $P_0$.
It was registered after development on separate statistics and scored once on \ExtCbRuns{} held-out runs ($M=1$--$4$, Gaussian or perturbed-identity factors; $M=1$ controls), with two verdict cells pooling $M=2$--$4$.
Both pass: with Gaussian factors error \ExtCbGaMae{} ($\ExtCbGaMaeCI$), within one in \ExtCbGaWithin{}; with perturbed-identity factors \ExtCbNiMae{} ($\ExtCbNiMaeCI$), within one in \ExtCbNiWithin{}.
Every registered baseline is worse with the paired CI above zero: the depth-one rule \ExtCbGaDone{} and \ExtCbNiDone{} (CIs $\ExtCbGaDoneCI$, $\ExtCbNiDoneCI$), the identity-factor race \ExtCbGaRaceId{} and \ExtCbNiRaceId{}, the static bound \ExtCbGaStatic{} and \ExtCbNiStatic{}, and the $\mu$ count of \citet{demelocosta2026drive} in its best variant (paired on the predictor) \ExtCbGaMu{} and \ExtCbNiMu{} (main-text form \ExtCbGaMuMain{} and \ExtCbNiMuMain{}).
Reported beside the verdict: the count read at $0.1T$ beats the rule with Gaussian factors (\ExtCbGaEarlyTen{}, difference CI $\ExtCbGaEarlyTenCI$) and loses with perturbed-identity factors (\ExtCbNiEarlyTen{}, CI $\ExtCbNiEarlyTenCI$); with Gaussian factors the gain over the depth-one rule is not resolved at a single depth ($M=2$: $\ExtCbGaMtwoDoneCI$; $M=3$: $\ExtCbGaMthreeDoneCI$), and at $M=4$ the rule does not beat the count read at $t=50$ ($\ExtCbGaMfourEarlyCI$).
\ExtCbFragile{} of the \ExtCbRuns{} runs are fragile under the truth floor; one single-depth cell (Gaussian, $M=2$) exceeds the registered 5\% and is flagged ill-posed, while both verdict cells are below it.
The frozen scorer was corrected once before any score was read: in runs with $T=500$ the early time $t=50$ coincides with $0.1T$ and was stored once; the fix reads that same-step count, and no run was repeated.

\paragraph{E8 and the ViT race (development).}
\Cref{fig:ext-e8} shows the per-epoch count of every E8 row; after a decrease T's lost directions keep shrinking at $\ExtEeightTdecay$ decades per unit of learning-rate time.
In development data that no test has scored, the per-direction drive and self-prediction parts of the linear model have reversed signs in most counted ViT directions during the first epochs, and early-training force readouts do not beat E3's step-0 rule; a race read instead from each direction's log headroom against the decoupled decay budget $2\sum_t\mathrm{lr}_t\,\mathrm{wd}$, with no fitted constant, places Arm T's weight-decay transition at \ExtSevenHeadLo--\ExtSevenHeadHi{} against \ExtSevenMeas{} measured (step-0 rule: \ExtSevenStep) and fails in Arm P.
It has since been registered as E7, with the $\mu$ count among its baselines, but not yet run, so we claim no ViT race.
In E8-R, a ramp of weight decay in Arm P, the count moves by $\ExtEeightRgap$ ($\ExtEeightRgapCI$), within the width ceiling, which is not informative.

